The '''Moviment del poble Birú''' (MPB; Volisanian for "Birú People's Movement") is a decentralised political activist movement in [[Balboa]] that advocates for [[Birú people|Birú]] rights, environmental protection, and opposition to infrastructure projects on indigenous lands. The movement has no formal leadership or hierarchical structure and is widely characterised as an {{w|anarcho-syndicalism|anarcho-syndicalist}} movement. The MPB emerged in the early 2020s in response to the expansion of {{w|data center}} construction in the [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]] highlands and has since become a prominent voice in Balboa's indigenous rights and environmental movements.

The MPB operates through local assemblies, affinity groups, and informal networks rather than through a centralised organisation. The movement has no spokesperson, no formal membership roster, and no headquarters. Decisions are made by consensus at open assemblies, and actions are coordinated through decentralised communication channels. The MPB works in coalition with mainstream leftist organisations, including the [[Socialist Front of Balboa]] (FSB), trade unions, and environmental groups, while maintaining its autonomy from any political party or institutional structure.

== Ideology ==

The MPB's political philosophy is rooted in {{w|anarcho-syndicalism}}, emphasising direct action, workers' self-organisation, and the rejection of hierarchical authority. The movement draws on the historical traditions of Balboan anarchism, particularly the [[Lliure Moviment de Treballadors]] (LMT) of the late nineteenth century, which organised among Fosian canal workers on the isthmus and advocated for the abolition of the wage system and the overthrow of the planter-commercial oligarchy.

The MPB's platform centres on three principal concerns. The first is the protection of [[Birú people|Birú]] land rights and the opposition to infrastructure projects — including hydroelectric dams, data centers, and mining operations — that encroach on traditional Birú lands in the Yalabirga highlands. The second is environmental protection, particularly the preservation of highland forests, water sources, and biodiversity in the Tramuntana range. The third is opposition to the fossil fuel energy infrastructure associated with data center construction, including the natural gas-fired power station funded by [[Noves Tecnologies d'Empreses]] in [[Portblanc]].

The movement's anarcho-syndicalist character is reflected in its organisational practices: the rejection of formal leadership, the use of consensus decision-making at open assemblies, and the emphasis on direct action over electoral politics. The MPB does not contest elections and does not endorse candidates, though its members participate in coalition actions with the FSB and other leftist organisations.

== History ==

The MPB emerged in the early 2020s from a network of Birú community assemblies, environmental groups, and leftist activists in the Tramuntana highlands. The movement's formation was prompted by the announcement of [[Noves Tecnologies d'Empreses]] (NTE) plans to construct a data center facility near [[Sant Jorge, Tramuntana|Sant Jorge]] on lands traditionally used by highland Birú communities. The proposed site was located in the [[La Serra de Llevant]] comarca, a region with a history of conflict over infrastructure development dating to the construction of the [[Gran Presa del Nord]] dam in the 1970s, which required the relocation of the Birú village of [[Vila de Llevant, Tramuntana|Vila de Llevant]].

The movement's early activities consisted of community assemblies, public information campaigns, and coordination with environmental organisations and the [[Socialist Front of Balboa]] (FSB). The MPB's decentralised structure allowed it to mobilise rapidly across the Tramuntana highlands and to coordinate with Birú communities in neighbouring comarcas without requiring a central command structure.

=== 2026 Balboa data center protests ===

{{main|2026 Balboa data center protests}}

The MPB played a central role in the nationwide protests of August 2026, which were triggered when NTE broke ground on the Sant Jorge facility on 18 August. The movement's decentralised network of assemblies and affinity groups enabled rapid mobilisation at the construction site and in cities across the country. The MPB coordinated with the FSB, trade unions, and environmental groups to organise demonstrations, roadblocks, and solidarity actions over the nine days of protests.

The movement's role in the protests drew national attention to the MPB and to the broader tensions between digital infrastructure development and indigenous land rights in the Tramuntana highlands. The protests ended on 27 August when the government announced that construction at the Sant Jorge site would be halted pending a review of the project's environmental and social impact.

== Structure and activities ==

The MPB has no formal membership, no leadership, and no headquarters. The movement operates through a network of local assemblies that meet in Birú communities, highland towns, and urban neighbourhoods. Each assembly is autonomous and sends delegates to regional coordination meetings, where decisions are made by consensus. There is no national executive or central committee.

The movement's activities include community organising, public information campaigns, direct action protests, and participation in coalition actions with mainstream leftist organisations. The MPB's direct actions have included roadblocks at construction sites, occupations of government buildings, and demonstrations at NTE facilities and shareholder meetings. The movement also engages in mutual aid projects in Birú communities, including food distribution, language revitalisation programmes, and community-based environmental monitoring.

The MPB maintains informal ties with the [[Socialist Front of Balboa]] and participates in coalition actions with the party, while explicitly rejecting any formal affiliation or endorsement. The movement's relationship with the FSB is pragmatic: the two organisations share common ground on environmental and indigenous rights issues, but the MPB's anarchist principles preclude participation in the party's electoral activities.

